Creating a Kubernetes cluster is a solved problem. What comes after — delivery,
secrets, certificates, tenancy, observability, backups — is not, and that is
where the cost lives.

So I built the part that comes after, as a kit you can fork:
[hcloud-k3s-platform-kit](https://github.com/sasin91/hcloud-k3s-platform-kit).
Then I ran it on real hardware, four times, until it stopped lying to me.

This post is the bill. Not an estimate.

::: note
**Part 1 of 7 of [Where to start with self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about building a platform on your own hardware and then arguing about whether to.

Previous: [Where to start with self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)

Next: [OpenTelemetry on a self-hosted k3s cluster](/blog/opentelemetry-on-k3s/)
:::

